
# MLComps

> Building reproducible, scalable, and collaboration-ready machine learning projects

**Notice:** We are currently in our initial development phase and **not accepting external contributions** at this time. We'll announce when we're ready to collaborate with the community!

---

## About Us

We are a team of recent Engineering graduates from **Carnegie Mellon University**, passionate about applying our knowledge in machine learning and artificial intelligence to real-world problems. MLComps serves as our playground for experimenting with ML models, developing best practices, and building production-ready solutions.

Our repositories are designed with reproducibility, scalability, and ease of deployment in mind, reflecting the rigorous engineering principles we learned at CMU.

**Status:** Just getting started! Building our foundation and establishing best practices. Contributions will be welcomed in the future - stay tuned!

---

##  Repository Standards

All repositories in this organization follow a unified structure to ensure consistency, reproducibility, and seamless deployment across different environments.

### Project Structure

Each repository adheres to the following standards:

#### **1. Script-Based Execution**
- All code is executable via command-line scripts with configurable arguments
- Designed for easy deployment on virtual machines (VMs) and cloud environments
- Clear entry points for training, inference, and evaluation

```bash
# Example usage
python scripts/train.py --config configs/default.yaml --epochs 100
python scripts/inference.py --model-path models/best.pt --input data/test/
```

#### **2. Notebook Integration**
- Interactive Jupyter notebooks provided for experimentation and rapid prototyping
- Notebooks are compatible with **Google Colab** and **Kaggle** environments
- Used primarily for exploratory data analysis, demonstrations, and critical testing scenarios
- Clear documentation on converting notebook workflows to production scripts

#### **3. Environment Management**
- We use **[uv](https://github.com/astral-sh/uv)** as our preferred Python package manager
- Fast, reliable dependency resolution and virtual environment management
- Each repository includes:
  - `pyproject.toml` for dependency specification
  - `.python-version` for Python version pinning
  - Setup instructions for quick environment bootstrapping

```bash
# Quick setup with uv
uv venv
uv pip install -e .
```

---

## 🛠️ Getting Started

### Prerequisites
- Python 3.9+ (specific version noted in each repository)
- [uv package manager](https://github.com/astral-sh/uv) (recommended)
- Git

### Installation
Each repository contains detailed setup instructions in its own README. Generally:

```bash
# Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/MLComps/repository-name.git
cd repository-name

# Set up environment with uv
uv venv
source .venv/bin/activate  # On Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
uv pip install -e .

# Run the project
python scripts/main.py --help
```

